#chapter[Introduction][
  Computation & complexity theory are two theories that focus on the possibility and the optimality of computation. They both study the aspect of computing objects and algorithms in two different ways: computation theory answers the questions that are related to the existence of algorithms and procedures, while complexity theory answers the questions that are related to the resources used by algorithms and theoretical limits for such usages.
]

#section[Some History]
The foundations of computation theory started around the $20^"th"$ century, as a mathematical question to understand the limits of mathematical reasoning. David Hilbert at the time had questions about general mathematical truths about the used logic fundamentals: are mathematics complete, consistent and decidable.

In 1936, multiple models for computation have been born, Alonzo Church's $lambda$-calculus, Alan Turing's Turing machines and Emil Post's Post machine. Those machines have really different descriptions, while are proven to be equivalent, that is, any computation doable in the one, it can be done in the others. Such proof lead to the Church-Turing thesis, which states in principle any computation can be done by a Turing machine.

Turing proved the existence of problems that cannot be solved by algorithms. One of such problems is the Halting Problem showing a clear limit for computation and giving rise to the theory of computability.

As the technology kept improving and computers became more practical in usual tasks around the 1950s, a new question became the focus of computer scientists, how efficient an algorithm for a task can be. Multiple algorithms can solve the same problem, but their efficiency is usually different, so, what is the efficiency limit for how can these be computed.

The focus of such discipline turned after Stephen Cook introduced the concept of NP-completeness in 1971, and proving that the SAT problem is NP-complete. Since then, the $"P" =^? "NP"$ question became a central open question in theoretical computer science which is unsolved until now (2026).

#section[Goals Of The Course]
In this course, our goal is to introduce as much concepts that were talked about here as possible, giving the mathematical foundation for computer science and goes through most questions that are related to these two beautiful theories.

Also, there will be multiple demonstrations and examples that will be available in my GitHub page, have fun, try discovering things. There will be also exercises and problem sheets taken from books and sources that would give a broader view on the domain, it would be my pleasure to check and help as much as possible.

